 "reading": "At 3e-4, where unit_density.json measured, the stack's cells fire about as often as the Transformer's units are open: on the test bed 42% of a level's cells and 42% of a block's units at a byte. That does not hold at the rates where the models train best. The stack's code thins as the peak rate rises. On the test bed 45%, 42%, 33%, 17% and 5% of a level's cells fire at a byte at 1.5e-4, 3e-4, 6e-4, 1.2e-3 and 2.4e-3 (the median level; the two seeds differ by 0.016 at most), and at full size 30%, 16% and 7% at 3e-4, 6e-4 and 1.2e-3. It thins with data as well: on the laptop's seed at 1.2e-3, 29% at 6,000 updates and 16% at 24,000, and the headline stack, trained at 3e-4 for 90,000 updates, fires at 18% where the 24,000-update pilot at that rate fires at 30%. The Transformer's units close far less: 48%, 42%, 36%, 32% and 26% are open over the same five rates on the test bed, and 35%, 30% and 26% at full size. And a closed unit still passes something, because a smooth gate lets a share through below zero: half of a block's units carry nine tenths of what the block passes on, at every rate and at both sizes (0.50 to 0.53 over all 14 Transformers). A silent cell passes nothing. Nine tenths of a level's spikes come from 31%, 29%, 23%, 11% and 3% of its cells on the test bed and from 20%, 11% and 4% at full size. At each model's best full-size rate that is 11% of the stack's cells against 51% of the Transformer's units. No cell dies: at full size none of 13,068 cells never fires at 3e-4 and at 6e-4, 2 at 1.2e-3 and 9 at 1.2e-3 under the lifting pool. The thinning is in what the synapses weigh and not in the cells' rests: with the rate a cell's resting charge falls (0.44, 0.40 and 0.33 of a threshold at full size), which alone would let it fire more often, while its input synapses grow (a median norm of 0.93, 1.51 and 2.46). The cells become detectors that large synapses hold silent at most bytes. The pool that lifts the ceiling and noisy thresholds leave the density as it is.",
 "how far the silent cells sit from their thresholds": "Counted on the laptop's seed of the test bed (1.2e-3). A cell fires from half a threshold of charge on, after its rest is taken off. After 24,000 updates 16% of cells and bytes are there in the median level, 1% lie within half a threshold below, 2% between zero and one threshold below zero, and 57% more than eight thresholds below zero. After 6,000 updates 29% fire and 35% lie more than eight thresholds below zero. A cell's resting charge is a third of a threshold. The silent cells are far from firing, and they move further with data: a lower rest, or a threshold that is soft over one threshold, would reach a few in a hundred of them.",
 "what it shows and what it does not": "The stack stops gaining from a higher rate where its code is thin. On the test bed its best rate is 1.2e-3, and at 2.4e-3, with 5% of its cells firing, it is 0.0042 worse, where the Transformer is 0.0039 better. At full size under the lifting pool it gains 0.0009 from 6e-4 to 1.2e-3, where the Transformer gains 0.0074. But the gap to the Transformer is no smaller at 3e-4, where about as many cells fire as units are open: 0.036 at full size at 3e-4 against 0.036 at each model's best rate under the lifting pool, and 0.059 against 0.048 on the test bed. So the measurement shows a difference between the two models that grows with the rate and with data, and does not show that it costs bits. No run held the density while the rate rose, and none let a silent cell pass anything.",
 "limits": "One seed at full size, and pilots of 24,000 updates on windows of 512 bytes. A unit that is open and a cell that fires are not the same measure, which is why the nine-tenths measure is given for both. The headline stack differs from the pilot at 3e-4 in its windows and its reads as well as its length. Nothing here is an intervention. The cells' charge was counted on the laptop's seed alone.",
