  "reading": "What the laptop's seed showed holds on every stack and grows with the rate. The silent cells sink: the share of cells and bytes more than eight thresholds below zero is 6%, 12%, 27%, 57% and 84% on the test bed at 1.5e-4, 3e-4, 6e-4, 1.2e-3 and 2.4e-3 (the two seeds within 0.03), and 34%, 63% and 86% at full size at 3e-4, 6e-4 and 1.2e-3 (63% and 83% under the lifting pool; 55% in the headline stack). The band within one threshold below firing shrinks with them: 11%, 9%, 7%, 3.5% and 1.2% on the test bed, 5.7%, 2.8% and 1.0% at full size, 3.5% in the headline stack. The cells' own spikes fall as the rate rises (test bed 15,000 to 3,100 a byte, full size 29,500 to 14,100) while the pulses a byte stay at the budget (111,400 to 113,500 on the test bed, 302,000 to 309,000 at full size): the reads and the output take what the cells give up. So at the rates where the models train best, a threshold that is soft over one threshold would reach one to three in a hundred cell-bytes at full size; a mechanism that is to keep the silent cells learning has to reach several thresholds below the rest, or hold the share of bytes a cell fires at.",
  "limits": "One seed at full size, as above. Charge is measured after the cell's rest is taken off, at the byte's end; a count says nothing about what the cell would learn if it fired."
