  "reading": "As the peak rate rises the cells' own spikes, the read pulses and the input's pulses fall, and the neurons that put a level's cells back on the stream send more, as do the reads' own cells. On the test bed from 1.5e-4 to 2.4e-3 (two-seed means): cells 14,570 to 3,105, read pulses 15,676 to 3,365; stream after cells 4,789 to 10,824, reads 24,170 to 33,730, output 36,702 to 48,203. At full size from 3e-4 to 1.2e-3: cells 29,531 to 14,098, read pulses 30,073 to 15,712, output 64,935 to 39,259; stream after cells 21,565 to 43,564, stream after reads 20,000 to 36,403, reads 111,106 to 121,594. So the output takes pulses on the test bed and gives them up at full size, where under the lifting pool it holds 58,369 at 6e-4 and 63,971 at 1.2e-3. The first amendment's sentence, that the reads and the output take what the cells give up, holds on the test bed alone. What holds at both sizes is that a cell's spike moves more of the stream as the rate rises: for every spike of a cell the stream after the cells sends 0.33, 0.44, 0.66, 1.36 and 3.49 pulses on the test bed over the five rates and 0.73, 1.55 and 3.09 at full size over its three. The same with data on the laptop's seed (output_step_on_trained_weights.json): 0.75 after 6,000 updates and 1.35 after 24,000. Fewer cells fire, and each spike weighs more.",
  "limits": "Counts on trained weights and no intervention. The pulses are counted as the trainer's pulse budget counts them; the pulses of the stream after the cells are those of its neurons, whatever moved them."
